WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.930
Our school bus visits nine

2
00:00:02.930 --> 00:00:04.950
villages every morning.

3
00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:07.930
Last week a student asked me two

4
00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:11.250
questions: is this really the shortest

5
00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:15.130
route, and why do I always get on first?

6
00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:19.310
Today: the bus route, built fast,

7
00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:21.630
improved, and judged fairly.

8
00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:25.650
Visiting every stop once and coming

9
00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:27.750
back by the shortest route is the

10
00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:29.430
travelling salesperson problem.

11
00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:32.260
One more village multiplies the

12
00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:35.140
routes: nine villages have a hundred

13
00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:37.740
and eighty-one thousand, and ten

14
00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:40.340
have one point eight million.

15
00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:44.160
Twenty have more than a billion billion.

16
00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:47.450
So start with a quick rule:

17
00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:49.570
always drive to the closest

18
00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:51.250
village you haven’t visited yet.

19
00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:54.780
It’s greedy: it never looks ahead, so

20
00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:56.860
the last legs home are often long.

21
00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:00.740
In Python, keep a list called route.

22
00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:03.840
While villages are left, append the

23
00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:05.820
one closest to your last stop.

24
00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:09.230
Then improve it with 2-opt.

25
00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:12.460
Pick two roads, reverse the stretch

26
00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:14.600
between them, and keep the change

27
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:15.820
if the route got shorter.

28
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.440
A crossing in a route always

29
00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:20.360
means a swap can shorten it.

30
00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:24.780
In code, a swap is a slice turned round.

31
00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:28.430
Try every pair of cut points, keep any

32
00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:30.990
change that helps, and stop when none

33
00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:31.250
does.

34
00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:33.020
Quick check!

35
00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:36.140
2-opt has stopped, but the lab

36
00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:38.100
says a shorter route exists.

37
00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:40.030
How can that be?

38
00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:43.600
No single swap helps, but a

39
00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:44.560
bigger change would.

40
00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:47.560
2-opt has found a local best,

41
00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:49.040
not the best of all.

42
00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:52.300
In the lab, three swaps take nearest

43
00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:54.380
neighbour’s thirty-four point six

44
00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:56.340
kilometres down to twenty-eight

45
00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.120
point six, the best route.

46
00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:01.130
On some new maps, it stops short.

47
00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:04.900
On the small map in your code, nearest

48
00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:06.840
neighbour drives nineteen kilometres.

49
00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:09.230
The best route is sixteen.

50
00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:13.450
Now look at the riders: the village picked

51
00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:15.790
up first rides almost the whole route.

52
00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:19.290
On the best route, driven one way, the

53
00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:21.310
longest ride is fifteen kilometres.

54
00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:24.050
Driven the other way, it’s fourteen.

55
00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:27.880
Same road, same length, a fairer

56
00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:30.380
ride, so check the direction too.

57
00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:34.130
In the app, open “The school bus route”.

58
00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:37.090
Press Run, and watch both steps.

59
00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:40.830
Then press New map until 2-opt

60
00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:42.550
stops short of the best.

61
00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:44.560
Your turn.

62
00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:47.200
Two routes have the same length.

63
00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:50.780
On one, a village rides for fifty

64
00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:53.280
minutes; on the other, nobody

65
00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:54.580
rides more than thirty.

66
00:02:55.210 --> 00:02:56.930
Which should the school choose?

67
00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:00.830
The one where nobody rides more than

68
00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:03.930
thirty: same cost, fairer ride.

69
00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:07.390
Remember: build a route fast with nearest

70
00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:10.230
neighbour, and improve it with 2-opt,

71
00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:12.290
which can stop at a local best.

72
00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:14.510
And judge a route by its

73
00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:16.570
length and its longest ride.

74
00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:20.400
Try it yourself: map five places you

75
00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:22.320
visit in a week, with rough distances.

76
00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:25.540
Plan a round trip by nearest neighbour,

77
00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:28.380
then look for a crossing and swap it.
